SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: let the stack send jumbo frames

The driver takes an MTU of 2030 at the most, because it receives into
2 KiB buffers. The receiver's export sends each 8 KiB slice of samples as
one UDP datagram, zero copy, which needs an MTU of 8236.

Raise the limit to 9194 for what the stack may send. Nothing else
changes: the receive buffers stay 2 KiB, so a frame larger than that is
not taken whole, and a port of this kernel must not be given a peer that
answers with jumbo frames it needs to hear.

Measured on an MT7988A's USXGMII ports (LTC Velo7 Max, RTL8261BE at
5 Gbit/s): frames of 1,900 to 9,000 bytes reach the peer whole and
valid, and a minute of slices at 4.5 Gbit/s arrives with none lost.

---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -5470,7 +5470,11 @@
 	if (MTK_HAS_CAPS(eth->soc->caps, MTK_SOC_MT7628))
 		eth->netdev[id]->max_mtu = MTK_MAX_RX_LENGTH - MTK_RX_ETH_HLEN;
 	else
-		eth->netdev[id]->max_mtu = MTK_MAX_RX_LENGTH_2K - MTK_RX_ETH_HLEN;
+		/* w1700k-sdr: the stack may send frames up to 9216 bytes (a slice of samples
+		 * is one datagram). Receiving is as it was: into 2 KiB buffers, so a larger
+		 * frame coming in is not taken whole.
+		 */
+		eth->netdev[id]->max_mtu = 9216 - MTK_RX_ETH_HLEN;
 
 	if (MTK_HAS_CAPS(eth->soc->caps, MTK_QDMA)) {
 		mac->device_notifier.notifier_call = mtk_device_event;
